<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charter Oath</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Charter Oath was a statement of five principles promulgated in 1868 at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji era</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the name of the young emperor. It promised deliberative assemblies, the participation of all classes, the abandonment of outdated customs and the seeking of knowledge throughout the world. It set out the programme of reform from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> emerged.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charter-oath.jpg" alt="Charter Oath" width="235" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of Japan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:5jo1.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first draft was written in January 1868 by the junior councillor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-kimimasa">Yuri Kimimasa</a>, in progressive language that spoke to the frustrations of radical but modestly born Meiji leaders. In February his colleague Fukuoka Takachika moderated it to be less alarming, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kido-takayoshi">Kido Takayoshi</a> prepared the final form, using language broad enough to embrace both readings. The Oath was meant both as a statement of policy for the government that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> and as an offer of inclusion to pro-Tokugawa domains, to keep them from joining the Tokugawa remnant in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Oath was promulgated on 6 April 1868 in the Kyoto Imperial Palace. Its five clauses promised that deliberative assemblies would be widely established and all matters decided by open discussion, that all classes would be united in carrying out the affairs of state, and that commoners, like officials, could pursue their own calling. Evil customs of the past were to be broken off and everything based on the just laws of Nature, and knowledge was to be sought throughout the world to strengthen imperial rule. Sanjō Sanetomi read it aloud before the Emperor and more than 400 officials, and 767 people signed in all.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The promised reforms were at first unfulfilled. A parliament with real power was not established until 1890, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a>, Chōshū, Tosa and Hizen kept political and military control well into the 20th century. The wording was deliberately broad, a promise of gradualism and equity: the reference to all classes implied that classes would continue, and only the search for knowledge pointed clearly to change, and that was to be selective and purposeful.</p><p>The Oath was repeated as the first article of the constitution of June 1868 and remained influential, more as inspiration than as a means of government. It can be considered the first constitution of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. After the Second World War Emperor Shōwa paid homage to it in his Imperial Rescript on National Revitalization.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charter Oath” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Meiji Restoration of January 1868 overthrew the Tokugawa shogunate after some 265 years and restored Emperor Meiji to nominal supreme power, while the <b>Charter Oath</b> of April 1868 committed Japan to seeking knowledge throughout the world and abandoning the customs of the feudal past.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Charter Oath</b>, issued on 6 April 1868, called for rejecting the old administration, enriching the country and adopting Western technology.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Shungaku worked for a peaceful transfer of power, and his retainer Yuri Kimimasa drafted the <b>Charter Oath</b> of 1868.</blockquote><a href="/state/fukui-domain">Fukui Domain</a></li></ul></section>
